    body: "A body has exactly one role. Dynamic bodies are moved by forces and contacts. Kinematic bodies move where you put them and push others without being pushed. Static bodies never move. Sensors report overlap without producing contact response. A body may participate in any number of constraints regardless of its role.\n\nThe collider is inferred from the shape and can be overridden in Physics Role. Closed, near-round freehand strokes infer solid circles; other closed strokes infer convex colliders. Path bodies choose between Bounding box, Bounding ellipse, Convex hull, and Path chain. A Path chain is a compound run of rounded solid segments usable for both fixed walls and moving bodies; its physical width follows the canvas stroke width, and overlapping round endcaps keep sharp turns watertight against small fast bodies.\n\nMaterials are friction, bounce, density, and damping, and they are what make a collision sound and feel different. Collision skin is an invisible per-object contact margin in scene pixels; the two skins add together, so it leaves a small visual gap while making fine, fast interactions stable. Keep it small, often 0.5 to 2 px. It is separate from Path chain thickness. Dynamic bodies run with continuous collision detection enabled, so fast objects are swept against their colliders rather than only tested at their endpoints.\n\nSelect several physics objects at once and Properties adds a Shared physics group. Mixed values read as Mixed until you author them; changing a shared field applies to every selected body in one update. This covers body type, enabled and sensor state, tags, collider, friction, bounce, density, damping, collision skin, and layer membership. Objects without a physics role are left untouched.\n\nTags are comma-separated labels on a body. Mappings and actuators filter by them, so tagging a group of bodies is usually easier than naming each one.",
